    {
      "question_idx": 4,
      "document_id": "6077605",
      "format": "pdf",
      "question": "What impact do alterations in mating patterns and distribution shifts have on the Gini index and Lorenz curves for income disparity in the years 1960 and 2005?",
      "answer": "In 2005, changing from assortative to random mating results in a significant drop in the Gini coefficient from 0.43 to 0.34, indicating reduced income inequality. However, in 1960, the change to random mating only slightly reduces the Gini coefficient from 0.34 to 0.33, showing minimal impact on income inequality. These differences are due to changes in marginal distributions and increased assortative mating over time.",
